{"channel": "code-review", "author": "dario", "created_at": "2025-02-10T11:30:00+00:00", "text": "Does the manual cost map override existing pricing data entirely, or does it only kick in as a fallback when we don't have pricing for that provider?", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1401"}
{"channel": "code-review", "author": "dario", "created_at": "2025-02-10T11:30:43+00:00", "text": "I'm guessing the cost map is keyed at the model level, not just provider-level?", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1402"}
{"channel": "code-review", "author": "dario", "created_at": "2025-02-10T11:30:51+00:00", "text": "Since costs vary per model even within a single provider", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1403"}
{"channel": "code-review", "author": "emil", "created_at": "2025-02-10T12:01:51+00:00", "text": "It overrides, the intent is you bring your own costs for providers we don't have data on, so falling back to our pricing would defeat the point", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1404"}
{"channel": "code-review", "author": "emil", "created_at": "2025-02-10T12:01:56+00:00", "text": "And yes, model-level keying", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1405"}
{"channel": "code-review", "author": "emil", "created_at": "2025-02-10T12:02:04+00:00", "text": "The part I'm less sure about is what happens when the map only covers some models for a provider and not others, worth a look when you're in the diff, Dario", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1406"}
{"channel": "code-review", "author": "emil", "created_at": "2025-02-10T12:02:46+00:00", "text": "hmm", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1407"}
{"channel": "code-review", "author": "emil", "created_at": "2025-02-10T12:21:46+00:00", "text": "@Dario - on that partial-coverage case I mentioned, if the cost map has some models for a provider but not others, should we error when we hit an unmapped model, or just skip cost tracking for it?", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1408"}
{"channel": "code-review", "author": "emil", "created_at": "2025-02-10T12:41:00+00:00", "text": "Ok, answering my own question, skip is probably the right call over erroring, otherwise adding a new model breaks existing workflows. But should we at least log a warning when that happens, or just silently track nothing?", "id": "m-code-review-2025-02-10-1409"}
{"channel": "engineering", "author": "emil", "created_at": "2025-02-10T12:44:43+00:00", "text": "Weekly just went out, check your inbox.", "id": "m-engineering-2025-02-10-1137", "reactions": [{"author": "dario", "emoji": "✅"}]}
{"channel": "engineering", "author": "emil", "created_at": "2025-02-10T12:44:51+00:00", "text": "@Dermot the revert was a pickler issue in online-request-processing, serialization was breaking things so we rolled back to the previous approach to keep post4 stable", "id": "m-engineering-2025-02-10-1138", "reactions": [{"author": "dario", "emoji": "👍"}]}
